confirmed: the kernel still lists /lab/7-a1/mnt/user/ao1 as mounted even though the process serving it is dead — reads against it fail (see results/ for the exact error text), rather than falling through to the catch-all's own view of that path or to whatever real directory happens to sit underneath. A dead per-share mount is a stuck, inaccessible path until it is explicitly unmounted — it does not silently expose the array beneath it, which matters because a stray write into a stale path cannot land anywhere by accident; it simply fails.
